      "Sometimes the answer is to train and promote existing employees. Sometimes it is to redesign roles. Sometimes a person and a role are no longer a good fit, and a respectful transition is appropriate. None of these outcomes should be pursued merely to demonstrate that the founder is growing.\n2.3 Build a stage-appropriate network\nA useful network is not simply a collection of impressive people. It is a set of relationships that helps you make better decisions and execute more effectively.",
      "Develop relationships across five groups:\n- Operators: people who have solved problems similar to yours.\n- Specialists: experts in areas such as finance, engineering, sales, hiring, or legal matters.\n- Peers: founders at a similar stage who understand the practical realities.\n- Customers: people who reveal what the market actually values.\n- Mentors and advisers: people who can challenge assumptions and help you see risks.\nDo not approach relationships as transactions. Build trust by being useful, prepared, honest, and consistent.\n2.4 Team evolution review\nEvery quarter, review each role—not just each individual.\nQuestion\tWhat it reveals\nIs the role still necessary?\tWhether the organisation has changed\nAre its responsibilities clear?\tWhether work is duplicated or neglected\nDoes the role match the person's strengths?\tWhether training or redesign could help\nWhat will the company need six to twelve months from now?\tFuture capability gaps\nCan the person grow into that need?\tDevelopment and succession opportunities\nIf not, what is a fair transition?\tResponsible workforce planning",
